{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>You shall not leave his corpse hanging overnight on the tree.</b> A warning not to leave the hanged man there overnight. From here the Sages, blessed be their memory, derived a warning against leaving the dead overnight; and likewise they said: 'One who leaves the dead overnight transgresses לא תלין,' for it says על העץ — 'on the tree': just as a tree is inedible (נוול), so too everything that is inedible (נוול); if he left him for his honor, it is permitted. And so the general principle of this commandment is that one who leaves the hanged man or the dead in the land of Israel transgresses two prohibitions — a negative and a positive commandment — on account of לא תלין and on account of the positive commandment of קבור תקברנו ('you shall surely bury him'). And it seems to me that since man's sin caused him not to have overnight lodging in the Garden of Eden — as it is written (Psalms 49:13): ״ואדם ביקר בל ילין״ — it would have been fitting that the Torah not be concerned about his being left overnight, but rather that he hang overnight and be degraded; but the Torah and the commandments are founded upon the pillars of wisdom, and the reason is explained in the verse <b>an outrage of Elohim is he who hangs.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it is a slight to the Holy One, blessed be He, if one leaves him hanging there, for he was hanged only for blasphemy or idol worship, and if one leaves him there everyone will say: 'Why is this one hanged? Because he cursed HaShem,' or 'because he worshipped idolatry in such-and-such a manner'; and public discourse about matters of idol worship is all a slight to the honor of the Omnipresent. If so, the word קללת embraces two meanings: cursing (קללה) and lightness/contempt (קלות). Thus too the Sages, blessed be their memory, explained in the chapter 'נגמר הדין': when a person suffers, what does his tongue say? ״קלני מראשי, קלני מזרועי״ — that is, the world is 'light' (קל) to him through his pain. And likewise the Shekhinah suffers over a suffering person and over the one who causes his death, as it is written (Exodus 3:7): ״כי ידעתי את מכאוביו״, and likewise (Isaiah 63:9): ״בכל צרתם לו צר״, and likewise (Leviticus 20:23): ״ואקוץ בם״ — from the language of thorns (קוצים); and this is קללת אלהים. And it says והעליתה ('and you shall take him down') — if so, let the Merciful One write קלת; what is the meaning of קללת? Conclude from it two things." "<b>And the sage.</b> R. Eliezer, of blessed memory, wrote that קללת אלהים תלוי — the curse of the Name takes effect upon a place near the hanged man, and it has a secret that cleaves to the soul; therefore ולא תטמא ('and you shall not defile')." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> כי קללת אלהים תלוי — a parable: to two brothers who resembled one another; one became king and one was seized as a bandit and hanged. One who sees him says, 'The king is hanged.' End." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> The illumination of the verse and the parable: the king is the Glory called צלם אלהים — that is, distinct from Him, that is, a servant of Elohim; and since the hanged man is in the likeness of צלם אלהים, behold he is קללת אלהים if he is not buried on his day and is left hanging there overnight, which is the time of the attribute of judgment. And what is understood from this is that there is no קללת אלהים if he is left there from morning until evening, provided he is not left overnight; and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:16.959865546Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}